            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Reads a file written by t.save, or by torch.save in a Python script: a tensor, or a dict or list of them. A model's weights file (a state_dict) comes out as a dict, one tensor per layer, named as the layers are named.\n\nIt reads safely: only tensors, numbers, strings and the dicts and lists that hold them. A file that would run code as it opens is refused. A runnable model file (TorchScript) is refused too, with a note to use t.model.\n\nLOADING:\nClick load and choose a file; it is read and sent at once. send sends it again\nwithout re-reading. A path in the path option, or sent as the message \"path <file>\",\nis read straight away - from a patch, with no dialog - and is read again when the patch opens.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.load <path> <device>\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nload (button, also an inlet):\nAsks for a file, then reads and sends it.\n\nsend (button, also an inlet):\nSends what was read again.\n\ndevice:\nWhere the tensors are put: cpu, or mps (the Mac's GPU) or cuda when there is one. Changing it moves what was read; send sends it from there.\n\npath:\nAn option, under the node: the file to read. A path that is not absolute is relative to the folder the app was started from, which is why the demo's path starts with dpg_system/help.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndata out:\nWhat the file holds: a tensor, or a dict (dict_keys lists its names, dict_extract takes out one).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nAt load the message reads the weights of the small network that the t.model page runs: six tensors, two per layer (weight and bias). dict_extract takes out the middle layer's weights, a 16 x 16 grid of how strongly each of the first layer's 16 units feeds each of the second's.\n\nRELATED:\nt.save writes the files. t.model runs a network saved with its code. np.load reads numpy's .npy and .npz files.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1020,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Reads a file written by t.save, or by torch.save in a Python script: a tensor, or a dict or list of them. A model's weights file (a state_dict) comes out as a dict, one tensor per layer, named as the layers are named.\n\nIt reads safely: only tensors, numbers, strings and the dicts and lists that hold them. A file that would run code as it opens is refused. A runnable model file (TorchScript) is refused too, with a note to use t.model.\n\nLOADING:\nClick load and choose a file; it is read and sent at once. send sends it again\nwithout re-reading. A path in the path option, or sent as the message \"path <file>\",\nis read straight away - from a patch, with no dialog - and is read again when the patch opens.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.load <path> <device>\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nload (button, also an inlet):\nAsks for a file, then reads and sends it.\n\nsend (button, also an inlet):\nSends what was read again.\n\ndevice:\nWhere the tensors are put: cpu, or mps (the Mac's GPU) or cuda when there is one. Changing it moves what was read; send sends it from there.\n\npath:\nAn option, under the node: the file to read. A path that is not absolute is relative to the folder the app was started from, which is why the demo's path starts with dpg_system/help.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndata out:\nWhat the file holds: a tensor, or a dict (dict_keys lists its names, dict_extract takes out one).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nAt load the message reads the weights of the small network that the t.model page runs: six tensors, two per layer (weight and bias). dict_extract takes out the middle layer's weights, a 16 x 16 grid of how strongly each of the first layer's 16 units feeds each of the second's.\n\nRELATED:\nt.save writes the files. t.model runs a network saved with its code. np.load reads numpy's .npy and .npz files."
        },
